  "title": "Joe Rogan Experience #392 - David Choe",
  "reviewed_on": "2026-07-31",
  "summary": "A 2013 conversation in which David Choe tests the limits of improvisational podcasting, explicit autobiography and risk. The episode begins with online identity, advertising and film before moving through a friend's threatening joke, incarceration fears, martial arts, painting fluency and Choe's anxiety about speaking publicly. Choe describes Snowman Monkey BBQ at Museo Universitario del Chopo, persistent visual phenomena after ayahuasca, MAD Symposium, Macau gambling and sexual-service encounters. The middle is dominated by sexuality, massage, consent, privacy, race and gender: direct stops, freezing, ambiguous assent, later enthusiasm, repeat visits and workers' possible reluctance remain separate evidence rather than one global label. A private Mexico partner's ambiguous anatomy joke produces months of speculation; the archive resolves none of it into intersex, transgender or male identity. Afghanistan travel, video games, Die Antwoord, accents and Belfast lead into a long proposed-fight sequence. Joe Rogan repeatedly warns about head trauma, refuses to train Choe casually and says not to fight; Choe says the fight probably will not happen, though a conditional year-long matched-preparation route is discussed. The final hour returns to Facebook stock, MAD/Noma, IBS, Korean-American food, gambling, creative discipline and the necessity of working regardless of inspiration. Every offensive, graphic, contradictory and uncertain idea remains. The editorial layer corrects words and public entities, separates advertising and factual context, protects private people, and publishes zero named line-level turns.",
